meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base
==========================

This layer contains the graphics-stack-independent RZ/G3L fast-boot work:

  Phase 1   Device-tree pruning and Linux kernel configuration trimming.
  Phase 2   Rootfs slimming and systemd startup-order optimisation.
  Phase 2b  Removal of the three remaining blocks on the boot critical path,
            each one located with systemd-analyze rather than guessed at.

It intentionally keeps the BSP's Weston graphics stack. Build
`core-image-weston` with the `rz-vlp-fast` distro to reproduce the baseline.
The separate `meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-qt-eglfs` layer adds phase 3 and replaces
Weston with Qt6/EGLFS.

Serial console silencing and bootdelay are U-Boot environment settings and are
not baked into either layer. See section V.

Phase 2b is documented in section VII, including how each figure was measured
and what to re-measure after a change.


I. What this layer changes
==========================

  Distro (conf/distro/rz-vlp-fast.conf)
    - derives from rz-vlp and pins linux-renesas 6.12
    - removes unused DISTRO_FEATURES and MACHINE_FEATURES
    - removes the RZ/VLP development and benchmark package groups
    - disables autostart for services whose features were removed
    - fixes SERIAL_CONSOLES to use ttySC3

  Kernel (recipes-kernel/linux/)
    - the DT patch disables unused network, USB, PCIe, CAN, camera, audio,
      extra UART, RTC and related peripheral nodes
    - fastboot.cfg removes unused kernel subsystems and drivers while keeping
      the display, boot storage and thermal-protection paths
    - fastboot.cfg also drops CONFIG_LEGACY_PTYS and the wireless subsystem,
      which exist only as device units for udev and systemd to walk (VII.1)

  Userspace
    - fastboot-systemd-tweaks.bbclass masks vconsole setup, the invalid ttyS0
      getty and systemd's network/identity services, and credits the saved
      random seed so sysinit.target stops waiting for the CRNG (VII.2)
    - systemd-conf_%.bbappend keeps journald in RAM and stops serial forwarding
    - weston-init.bbappend lets Weston start in parallel with multi-user.target
      and runs the compositor directly instead of inside a logind session,
      which removes user@998.service from the critical path (VII.3)


II. Required layers
===================

Use a standard RZ/G3L VLP project with at least:

  poky/meta
  poky/meta-poky
  poky/meta-yocto-bsp
  meta-openembedded/meta-oe
  meta-openembedded/meta-python
  meta-openembedded/meta-multimedia
  meta-arm/meta-arm-toolchain
  meta-arm/meta-arm
  meta-renesas/meta-rz-bsp
  meta-renesas/meta-rz-distro
  meta-rz-features/meta-rz-codecs
  meta-rz-features/meta-rz-graphics
  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base

This layer has priority 20 and must follow the Renesas BSP, distro and graphics
layers in bblayers.conf.

Tested with Yocto scarthgap, RZ/G3L BSP v1.0.0-rc1 and linux-renesas
6.12.46-cip8.


III. Build the phase-2 Weston baseline
======================================

  $ source poky/oe-init-build-env build
  $ bitbake-layers add-layer ../meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base

Set in conf/local.conf:

  MACHINE = "smarc-rzg3l"
  DISTRO  = "rz-vlp-fast"

Then build:

  $ bitbake core-image-weston

Useful metadata checks:

  $ bitbake-layers show-layers
  $ bitbake-layers show-appends
  $ bitbake -e core-image-weston | \
        grep -E '^(DISTRO|PREFERRED_VERSION_linux-renesas|SERIAL_CONSOLES)='


IV. Add the Qt/EGLFS phase
==========================

Do not copy the base files into another layer. Add the overlay after this layer:

  meta-qt6
  meta-rz-qt6
  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-base
  meta-rzg3l-fast-boot-qt-eglfs

Then select:

  DISTRO = "rz-vlp-fast-qt-eglfs"

and build:

  $ bitbake core-image-hmi


V. U-Boot environment
=====================

The stock bootcmd resets the environment before loading the image:

  bootcmd = env default -a; run bootcmd_check; run bootimage

Remove that reset before saving fast-boot settings:

  => setenv bootcmd 'run bootcmd_check; run bootimage'
  => setenv bootdelay 0
  => setenv prodemmcbootargs 'setenv bootargs rw rootwait root=/dev/mmcblk0p2 quiet loglevel=4 systemd.show_status=0'
  => setenv prodsdbootargs  'setenv bootargs rw rootwait root=/dev/mmcblk1p2 quiet loglevel=4 systemd.show_status=0'
  => saveenv
  => reset

Dropping earlycon avoids replaying the kernel ring buffer synchronously over
the 115200-baud UART. Re-add earlycon when debugging an early kernel failure.


VI. Product constraints
=======================

The DT profile disables Ethernet, USB, PCIe, CAN, camera, audio, i2c0/i2c1/
i2c3, extra UARTs and RTC. Restore both the DT nodes and their matching kernel
and userspace features before adding any of those product functions.

The microSD controller stays enabled for the development boot loop. A fixed
eMMC product can disable it after manufacturing and recovery requirements are
settled.

Phase 2b adds two constraints:

  - Weston runs as root, without a logind session. VT switching is gone. See
    VII.3 for how to revert just this if a product needs a non-root compositor.
  - systemd's resolved, networkd, timesyncd and userdbd are masked. Adding any
    network function back means un-masking them together with the DT node and
    the DISTRO_FEATURES entry.


VII. Phase 2b: the boot critical path
=====================================

Phase 2 left userspace at roughly 75% of the boot. `systemd-analyze plot`
showed why: the path from systemd to graphical.target is not a broad parallel
fan-out with some slow services in it, it is six strictly serial segments.
Because they are serial, the savings from removing them add up.

Baseline (RZ/G3L EVK, phase 2 + meta-rzg3l-fast-logo, eMMC boot):

  systemd-analyze -> 553ms (kernel) + 6.284s (userspace) = 6.837s
                     graphical.target reached after 6.176s in userspace

  segment                                    kernel-rel     cost
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
  kernel -> systemd runnable                 0     .. 0.553   553 ms
  systemd start-up + sysinit.target          0.553 .. 2.757  2204 ms   VII.2
  basic.target / sockets / user-sessions      2.757 .. 2.883   126 ms
  512 legacy PTY device units                2.883 .. 3.997  1114 ms   VII.1
  getty.target / multi-user.target           3.997 .. 4.069    72 ms
  weston.service                             4.069 .. 6.727  2658 ms   VII.3
  ---------------------------------------------------------------
  graphical.target                                   6.729

VII.1 to VII.3 remove one segment each. Because the segments are serial the
estimates add: roughly -1.1 s, -0.6 s and -2.1 s, so graphical.target should
land near 2.9 s. Those are projections from the baseline plot, not measured
results - re-measure per VII.4 and correct this table.

`systemd-analyze plot` uses a kernel-relative x axis; `critical-chain` prints
userspace-relative `@` times. The two differ by exactly the 553 ms kernel
phase. All figures in this section are kernel-relative, so they line up with
dmesg timestamps.

Do not use the serial `login:` prompt as the metric. serial-getty is Type=idle,
so it deliberately waits for the job queue to drain - in the baseline it prints
at 7.897s, 1.2 s after the boot has actually finished.


VII.1 512 legacy PTY device units - 1114 ms
-------------------------------------------

CONFIG_LEGACY_PTYS creates 256 BSD-style pty pairs. udev emits an event per
device and systemd instantiates two device units for each, so the boot
transaction carries 512 units that nothing will ever open - 81% of every unit
in the plot. systemd walks them serially, and that walk lands squarely between
systemd-user-sessions.service finishing (2.980s) and multi-user.target /
weston.service being allowed to start (4.055s / 4.069s).

The same 512 udev events are also most of the work in
systemd-udev-trigger.service (1.049s), and the reason dev-mmcblk0p2.device
does not settle until 2.757s.

Fixed in fastboot.cfg by dropping CONFIG_LEGACY_PTYS. CONFIG_UNIX98_PTYS
(/dev/ptmx) is asserted positively in the same fragment so a defconfig change
can never leave the board with no pty support at all.

Note the Image does not get smaller: legacy ptys are only a few KB of code.
The win here is unit count, not size.

The wireless subsystem goes at the same time: rz-vlp-fast already removes wifi
and bluetooth from DISTRO_FEATURES, but the kernel still built cfg80211/mac80211
in - visible in the boot log as the regulatory.db load failing - and rfkill
added two more device units. CONFIG_WLAN has to be the one disabled;
drivers/net/wireless/Kconfig does "select WIRELESS" and WLAN is default y, so
turning off WIRELESS alone is silently reverted by kconfig. Always verify a
negative fragment entry against the generated .config (VII.4) rather than
assuming it took.


VII.2 sysinit.target - 2204 ms
------------------------------

`critical-chain` names systemd-resolved.service as the last link before
sysinit.target, but the plot shows four services finishing within 110 ms of
each other, all of them gating the same target:

  systemd-timesyncd      1.886 .. 2.556   669 ms
  systemd-random-seed    1.623 .. 2.622   998 ms   <- crng init done @2.590
  systemd-resolved       1.873 .. 2.640   766 ms   <- on the critical chain
  systemd-networkd       1.926 .. 2.665   739 ms
  sysinit.target                 2.757

So both causes have to be dealt with in the same step; removing either one
alone just promotes the other to be the new gate.

  Network and identity services. The DT profile has no Ethernet, so networkd,
  resolved, timesyncd, userdbd and network-generator have nothing to do -
  2.51 service-seconds of pure waste, and resolved is on the chain. They ship
  inside the systemd recipe, so SYSTEMD_AUTO_ENABLE cannot reach them; they are
  masked in the rootfs instead. See FASTBOOT_MASKED_UNITS.

  Entropy. systemd-random-seed is not slow, it is blocked: after writing the
  saved seed to /dev/urandom it generates a fresh one to write back, and that
  blocks in getrandom() until the CRNG initialises. Crediting the seed
  initialises the CRNG immediately, so the second half stops blocking. The
  class comment records why crediting is safe on this image and what the
  first-boot-after-flash caveat is.

  There is no kernel option for the first-boot-after-flash case. 6.12 removed
  CONFIG_RANDOM_TRUST_BOOTLOADER; drivers/char/random.c now defines
  "trust_bootloader __initdata = true" and only random.trust_bootloader= on the
  command line turns it off, so the kernel already credits a bootloader seed.
  The outstanding work is entirely on the U-Boot side - publishing
  /chosen/rng-seed. fastboot.cfg does drop CONFIG_RANDOMIZE_BASE, which the
  boot log shows was never taking effect anyway.

Step VII.1 helps here as well: dev-mmcblk0p2.device settles at 2.757s, exactly
when sysinit.target completes, because udev's queue is clogged with the 512
pty events.


VII.3 weston.service - 2658 ms
------------------------------

Stock weston.service runs the compositor as User=weston with
PAMName=weston-autologin. pam_systemd asks logind to create a session, and
logind brings up a complete per-user service manager for uid 998. Lining up
`blame`, the plot and the dmesg audit records:

  4.069s  systemd forks the weston executor, PAM starts
  4.325s  user@998.service starts
  6.153s  user@998.service ready                    (blame: 1.827s)
  6.187s  loginuid set, PAM session established     (audit, comm="(weston)")
  6.727s  weston active -> graphical.target

2118 ms of the 2658 ms is session setup. The compositor itself needs 540 ms.

weston-init.bbappend therefore drops User=, Group=, PAMName= and the
systemd-user-sessions.service dependency, so the compositor runs directly with
no logind session and no user manager.

The Wayland socket is unaffected: weston.socket keeps SocketUser=weston and
keeps listening on /run/wayland-0, weston inherits the descriptor, and
/etc/profile.d/weston-socket.sh keeps pointing clients at that absolute path.
Nothing ever referenced /run/user/998. The weston user and its input/video/
render group memberships stay in place because weston.socket still needs the
user to exist. weston does still want XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, so the bbappend gives
it a private /run/weston through RuntimeDirectory=.

systemd-logind.service is deliberately *not* masked: the serial console login
still goes through pam_systemd, and logind is off the critical path anyway
(2.841s .. 3.137s, in parallel).

This is the one step in phase 2b with a trade-off rather than a pure removal.
The compositor now runs as root and uses weston's launcher-direct backend
instead of the logind backend; the capability actually lost is VT switching,
which has no user on this board. If a product cannot accept a root compositor,
revert this bbappend hunk - VII.1 and VII.2 are independent of it. The failure
mode if the assumption is wrong is loud (weston does not start,
graphical.target times out), never silent.

The bbappend asserts that every line it strips is present and calls bbfatal if
not, so a weston-init update cannot quietly reintroduce the login session and
the 2.1 s with it.


VII.4 Re-measuring
------------------

Check the kernel side on the build host first - a negative fragment entry can
be silently reverted by a "select" from another symbol, or name a symbol that
no longer exists:

  $ bitbake -c kernel_configcheck -f linux-renesas
  $ C=$(find tmp/work/*/linux-renesas/*/linux-*-build -name .config)
  $ grep -E '^(CONFIG_X=|# CONFIG_X is not set)' $C   # for each X in fastboot.cfg

Then on the target:

  systemd-analyze
  systemd-analyze blame
  systemd-analyze critical-chain graphical.target
  systemd-analyze plot > /boot.svg

Read the numbers from `systemd-analyze`, not from the serial log. Take ten
boots and record the P95: systemd-random-seed.service blocks for a variable
time (VII.2), so a single best-case boot is not representative.

When measuring anything that logs at info level, remember that
systemd-conf_%.bbappend sets MaxLevelStore=warning - raise it to info for the
measurement boot or the records will not be in the journal.
